{"question_text": {"Medicine": [{"id": "1", "question": "১) Which component of vitamin B complex doesn't show neurological manifestation?", "option1": "thiamine", "option2": "riboflavin", "option3": "niacin", "option4": "pyridoxine", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"216\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"100\">Riboflavin (Vitamin B₂) deficiency</strong><span> </span>primarily affects the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"123\" data-end=\"152\">skin and mucous membranes</strong><span> </span>and<span> </span><strong data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"215\">does not typically produce neurological manifestations</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"260\">Features of riboflavin deficiency include:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"261\" data-end=\"371\">\r\n<li data-start=\"261\" data-end=\"280\">\r\n<p data-start=\"263\" data-end=\"280\">Angular cheilitis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"292\">\r\n<p data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"292\">Cheilosis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"293\" data-end=\"321\">\r\n<p data-start=\"295\" data-end=\"321\">Glossitis (magenta tongue)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"322\" data-end=\"345\">\r\n<p data-start=\"324\" data-end=\"345\">Seborrheic dermatitis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"371\">\r\n<p data-start=\"348\" data-end=\"371\">Corneal vascularization</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"373\" data-end=\"439\">The other options are associated with neurological manifestations:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"740\">\r\n<li data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"543\">\r\n<p data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"543\"><strong data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"467\">Thiamine (Vitamin B₁)</strong><span> </span>– peripheral neuropathy,<span> </span><strong data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"509\">dry beriberi</strong>,<span> </span><strong data-start=\"511\" data-end=\"542\">Wernicke–Korsakoff syndrome</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"544\" data-end=\"632\">\r\n<p data-start=\"546\" data-end=\"632\"><strong data-start=\"546\" data-end=\"569\">Niacin (Vitamin B₃)</strong><span> </span>– causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"579\" data-end=\"591\">pellagra</strong>: dermatitis, diarrhea, and<span> </span><strong data-start=\"619\" data-end=\"631\">dementia</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"633\" data-end=\"740\">\r\n<p data-start=\"635\" data-end=\"740\"><strong data-start=\"635\" data-end=\"662\">Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆)</strong><span> </span>– deficiency can cause<span> </span><strong data-start=\"686\" data-end=\"711\">peripheral neuropathy</strong>, seizures, and irritability.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "2", "question": "২) Which one is found only in foods of animal origin ?", "option1": "folic acid", "option2": "pyridoxine", "option3": "cobalamin", "option4": "thiamine", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"419\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"89\">Vitamin B₁₂ (cobalamin)</strong><span> </span>is found<span> </span><strong data-start=\"99\" data-end=\"140\">exclusively in foods of animal origin</strong>, such as<span> </span><strong data-start=\"150\" data-end=\"197\">meat, liver, fish, eggs, and dairy products</strong>. It is synthesized by microorganisms and is absent from natural plant foods. Therefore, strict vegetarians and vegans are at increased risk of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"341\" data-end=\"367\">vitamin B₁₂ deficiency</strong><span> </span>unless they consume fortified foods or supplements.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"421\" data-end=\"461\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"462\" data-end=\"780\">\r\n<li data-start=\"462\" data-end=\"555\">\r\n<p data-start=\"464\" data-end=\"555\"><strong data-start=\"464\" data-end=\"491\">Folic acid (Vitamin B₉)</strong><span> </span>is abundant in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"507\" data-end=\"554\">green leafy vegetables, legumes, and fruits</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"556\" data-end=\"677\">\r\n<p data-start=\"558\" data-end=\"677\"><strong data-start=\"558\" data-end=\"585\">Pyridoxine (Vitamin B₆)</strong><span> </span>is found in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"598\" data-end=\"629\">both plant and animal foods</strong>, including cereals, vegetables, meat, and fish.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"678\" data-end=\"780\">\r\n<p data-start=\"680\" data-end=\"780\"><strong data-start=\"680\" data-end=\"705\">Thiamine (Vitamin B₁)</strong><span> </span>is widely distributed in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"731\" data-end=\"779\">whole grains, legumes, nuts, pork, and yeast</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "3", "question": "৩) Hypothermia occurs when core temperature falls below ___oC?", "option1": "37", "option2": "35", "option3": "32", "option4": "28", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"43\" data-end=\"140\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"43\" data-end=\"58\">Hypothermia</strong><span> </span>is defined as a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"75\" data-end=\"118\">core body temperature below 35°C (95°F)</strong>. It is classified as:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"141\" data-end=\"204\">\r\n<li data-start=\"141\" data-end=\"160\">\r\n<p data-start=\"143\" data-end=\"160\"><strong data-start=\"143\" data-end=\"152\">Mild:</strong><span> </span>32–35°C</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"161\" data-end=\"184\">\r\n<p data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"184\"><strong data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"176\">Moderate:</strong><span> </span>28–32°C</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"185\" data-end=\"204\">\r\n<p data-start=\"187\" data-end=\"204\"><strong data-start=\"187\" data-end=\"198\">Severe:</strong><span> </span>&lt;28°C</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"206\" data-end=\"246\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"247\" data-end=\"421\">\r\n<li data-start=\"247\" data-end=\"294\">\r\n<p data-start=\"249\" data-end=\"294\"><strong data-start=\"249\" data-end=\"257\">37°C</strong><span> </span>is the normal core body temperature.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"295\" data-end=\"376\">\r\n<p data-start=\"297\" data-end=\"376\"><strong data-start=\"297\" data-end=\"305\">32°C</strong><span> </span>indicates<span> </span><strong data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"340\">moderate hypothermia</strong>, not the threshold for hypothermia.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"377\" data-end=\"421\">\r\n<p data-start=\"379\" data-end=\"421\"><strong data-start=\"379\" data-end=\"387\">28°C</strong><span> </span>indicates<span> </span><strong data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"420\">severe hypothermia</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "4", "question": "৪) Which is not a manifestation of iodine deficiency in adult?", "option1": "goiter", "option2": "hypothyroidism", "option3": "cretinism", "option4": "myxoedema", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"48\" data-end=\"98\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"48\" data-end=\"79\">Iodine deficiency in adults</strong><span> </span>commonly leads to:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"99\" data-end=\"311\">\r\n<li data-start=\"99\" data-end=\"154\">\r\n<p data-start=\"101\" data-end=\"154\"><strong data-start=\"101\" data-end=\"111\">Goiter</strong><span> </span>– due to compensatory thyroid enlargement.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"155\" data-end=\"230\">\r\n<p data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"230\"><strong data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"175\">Hypothyroidism</strong><span> </span>– resulting from inadequate thyroid hormone synthesis.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"231\" data-end=\"311\">\r\n<p data-start=\"233\" data-end=\"311\"><strong data-start=\"233\" data-end=\"246\">Myxoedema</strong><span> </span>– the clinical manifestation of severe hypothyroidism in adults.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"617\"><strong data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"326\">Cretinism</strong><span> </span>is<span> </span><strong data-start=\"330\" data-end=\"337\">not</strong><span> </span>a manifestation of iodine deficiency in adults. It occurs in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"399\" data-end=\"422\">fetuses and infants</strong><span> </span>due to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"430\" data-end=\"462\">congenital iodine deficiency</strong><span> </span>or congenital hypothyroidism and is characterized by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"516\" data-end=\"616\">severe intellectual disability, growth retardation, deaf-mutism, and delayed skeletal maturation</strong>.</p>"}, {"id": "5", "question": "৫) Which one of the following is acute effect of radiation exposure?", "option1": "infertility", "option2": "cataract", "option3": "teratogenesis", "option4": "bone necrosis", "answer": "1", "exp": "<p data-start=\"50\" data-end=\"318\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"50\" data-end=\"89\">Acute effects of ionizing radiation</strong><span> </span>occur within hours to weeks after significant exposure and involve rapidly dividing cells. The<span> </span><strong data-start=\"185\" data-end=\"221\">gonads are highly radiosensitive</strong>, and radiation can cause<span> </span><strong data-start=\"247\" data-end=\"285\">temporary or permanent infertility</strong>, depending on the dose received.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"320\" data-end=\"382\">The other options are<span> </span><strong data-start=\"342\" data-end=\"368\">late (delayed) effects</strong><span> </span>of radiation:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"659\">\r\n<li data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"440\">\r\n<p data-start=\"385\" data-end=\"440\"><strong data-start=\"385\" data-end=\"397\">Cataract</strong><span> </span>– develops months to years after exposure.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"441\" data-end=\"546\">\r\n<p data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"546\"><strong data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"460\">Teratogenesis</strong><span> </span>– occurs after fetal exposure during pregnancy and is a delayed developmental effect.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"547\" data-end=\"659\">\r\n<p data-start=\"549\" data-end=\"659\"><strong data-start=\"549\" data-end=\"566\">Bone necrosis</strong><span> </span>– a late complication of radiotherapy due to chronic tissue damage and impaired vascularity.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "6", "question": "৬) What is the manifestation of niacin deficiency?", "option1": "beriberi", "option2": "scurvy", "option3": "pellagra", "option4": "glossitis", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"47\" data-end=\"154\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"47\" data-end=\"81\">Niacin (Vitamin B₃) deficiency</strong><span> </span>causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"89\" data-end=\"101\">pellagra</strong>, which is classically characterized by the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"153\">4 Ds</strong>:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"155\" data-end=\"245\">\r\n<li data-start=\"155\" data-end=\"188\">\r\n<p data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"188\"><strong data-start=\"157\" data-end=\"171\">Dermatitis</strong><span> </span>(photosensitive)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"189\" data-end=\"203\">\r\n<p data-start=\"191\" data-end=\"203\"><strong data-start=\"191\" data-end=\"203\">Diarrhea</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"204\" data-end=\"218\">\r\n<p data-start=\"206\" data-end=\"218\"><strong data-start=\"206\" data-end=\"218\">Dementia</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"219\" data-end=\"245\">\r\n<p data-start=\"221\" data-end=\"245\"><strong data-start=\"221\" data-end=\"230\">Death</strong><span> </span>(if untreated)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"247\" data-end=\"311\">The other options are due to deficiencies of different vitamins:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"597\">\r\n<li data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"373\">\r\n<p data-start=\"314\" data-end=\"373\"><strong data-start=\"314\" data-end=\"326\">Beriberi</strong><span> </span>– due to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"336\" data-end=\"372\">thiamine (Vitamin B₁) deficiency</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"421\">\r\n<p data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"421\"><strong data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"386\">Scurvy</strong><span> </span>– due to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"420\">vitamin C deficiency</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"422\" data-end=\"597\">\r\n<p data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"597\"><strong data-start=\"424\" data-end=\"437\">Glossitis</strong><span> </span>– may occur with deficiencies of several vitamins (e.g.,<span> </span><strong data-start=\"495\" data-end=\"530\">riboflavin, folate, vitamin B₁₂</strong>) and is not the characteristic manifestation of niacin deficiency.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "7", "question": "৭) Which one corresponds to Bitot's spot in WHO classification of Xeropthalmia?", "option1": "X1A", "option2": "X1B", "option3": "X2", "option4": "X3B", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"42\" data-end=\"99\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\">According to the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"59\" data-end=\"98\">WHO classification of xerophthalmia</strong>:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"101\" data-end=\"429\">\r\n<li data-start=\"101\" data-end=\"127\">\r\n<p data-start=\"103\" data-end=\"127\"><strong data-start=\"103\" data-end=\"109\">XN</strong><span> </span>– Night blindness</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"128\" data-end=\"160\">\r\n<p data-start=\"130\" data-end=\"160\"><strong data-start=\"130\" data-end=\"137\">X1A</strong><span> </span>– Conjunctival xerosis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"161\" data-end=\"190\">\r\n<p data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"190\"><strong data-start=\"163\" data-end=\"170\">X1B</strong><span> </span>–<span> </span><strong data-start=\"173\" data-end=\"190\">Bitot's spots</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"191\" data-end=\"217\">\r\n<p data-start=\"193\" data-end=\"217\"><strong data-start=\"193\" data-end=\"199\">X2</strong><span> </span>– Corneal xerosis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"295\">\r\n<p data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"295\"><strong data-start=\"220\" data-end=\"227\">X3A</strong><span> </span>– Corneal ulceration/keratomalacia involving<span> </span><strong data-start=\"273\" data-end=\"281\">&lt;1/3</strong><span> </span>of the cornea</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"296\" data-end=\"373\">\r\n<p data-start=\"298\" data-end=\"373\"><strong data-start=\"298\" data-end=\"305\">X3B</strong><span> </span>– Corneal ulceration/keratomalacia involving<span> </span><strong data-start=\"351\" data-end=\"359\">&gt;1/3</strong><span> </span>of the cornea</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"397\">\r\n<p data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"397\"><strong data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"382\">XS</strong><span> </span>– Corneal scar</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"429\">\r\n<p data-start=\"400\" data-end=\"429\"><strong data-start=\"400\" data-end=\"406\">XF</strong><span> </span>– Xerophthalmic fundus</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"431\" data-end=\"589\"><strong data-start=\"431\" data-end=\"448\">Bitot's spots</strong><span> </span>are<span> </span><strong data-start=\"453\" data-end=\"491\">foamy, whitish, triangular patches</strong><span> </span>on the bulbar conjunctiva caused by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"528\" data-end=\"552\">vitamin A deficiency</strong><span> </span>and correspond to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"571\" data-end=\"588\">WHO stage X1B</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"591\" data-end=\"631\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"632\" data-end=\"761\">\r\n<li data-start=\"632\" data-end=\"664\">\r\n<p data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"664\"><strong data-start=\"634\" data-end=\"641\">X1A</strong><span> </span>= Conjunctival xerosis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"665\" data-end=\"691\">\r\n<p data-start=\"667\" data-end=\"691\"><strong data-start=\"667\" data-end=\"673\">X2</strong><span> </span>= Corneal xerosis</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"692\" data-end=\"761\">\r\n<p data-start=\"694\" data-end=\"761\"><strong data-start=\"694\" data-end=\"701\">X3B</strong><span> </span>= Keratomalacia involving more than one-third of the cornea</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "8", "question": "৮) Tender, red or purplish skin lesions that occur in the cold and wet environment is termed as........?", "option1": "immersion foot", "option2": "chilblains", "option3": "trench foot", "option4": "frostbite", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"57\" data-end=\"333\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"57\" data-end=\"80\">Chilblains (pernio)</strong><span> </span>are<span> </span><strong data-start=\"85\" data-end=\"145\">tender, itchy, red or purplish inflammatory skin lesions</strong><span> </span>that develop after exposure to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"177\" data-end=\"222\">cold, damp (wet), non-freezing conditions</strong>. They most commonly affect the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"254\" data-end=\"287\">fingers, toes, ears, and nose</strong><span> </span>due to an abnormal vascular response to cold.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"335\" data-end=\"375\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"763\">\r\n<li data-start=\"376\" data-end=\"506\">\r\n<p data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"506\"><strong data-start=\"378\" data-end=\"396\">Immersion foot</strong><span> </span>results from prolonged exposure of the feet to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"444\" data-end=\"458\">cold water</strong>, causing swelling, numbness, and tissue damage.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"507\" data-end=\"640\">\r\n<p data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"640\"><strong data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"524\">Trench foot</strong><span> </span>is a type of immersion foot caused by prolonged exposure to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"585\" data-end=\"609\">cold, wet conditions</strong>, primarily affecting the feet.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"641\" data-end=\"763\">\r\n<p data-start=\"643\" data-end=\"763\"><strong data-start=\"643\" data-end=\"656\">Frostbite</strong><span> </span>occurs due to exposure to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"683\" data-end=\"708\">freezing temperatures</strong>, leading to freezing of tissues and possible necrosis.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "9", "question": "৯) The energy provided by 1g of protein is equivalent to which of the following?", "option1": "ক) 16 kJ/g", "option2": "খ) 37 kJ/g", "option3": "গ) 17 kJ/g", "option4": "ঘ) 29 kJ/g", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"46\" data-end=\"183\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"46\" data-end=\"99\">Protein provides approximately 17 kJ/g (4 kcal/g)</strong><span> </span>of energy. This is one of the standard physiological fuel values used in nutrition.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"185\" data-end=\"200\">For comparison:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"201\" data-end=\"353\">\r\n<li data-start=\"201\" data-end=\"238\">\r\n<p data-start=\"203\" data-end=\"238\"><strong data-start=\"203\" data-end=\"215\">Protein:</strong><span> </span><strong data-start=\"216\" data-end=\"238\">17 kJ/g (4 kcal/g)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"239\" data-end=\"281\">\r\n<p data-start=\"241\" data-end=\"281\"><strong data-start=\"241\" data-end=\"258\">Carbohydrate:</strong><span> </span><strong data-start=\"259\" data-end=\"281\">17 kJ/g (4 kcal/g)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"315\">\r\n<p data-start=\"284\" data-end=\"315\"><strong data-start=\"284\" data-end=\"292\">Fat:</strong><span> </span><strong data-start=\"293\" data-end=\"315\">37 kJ/g (9 kcal/g)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"316\" data-end=\"353\">\r\n<p data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"353\"><strong data-start=\"318\" data-end=\"330\">Alcohol:</strong><span> </span><strong data-start=\"331\" data-end=\"353\">29 kJ/g (7 kcal/g)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"395\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"562\">\r\n<li data-start=\"396\" data-end=\"466\">\r\n<p data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"466\"><strong data-start=\"398\" data-end=\"409\">16 kJ/g</strong><span> </span>is slightly lower than the standard physiological value.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"467\" data-end=\"512\">\r\n<p data-start=\"469\" data-end=\"512\"><strong data-start=\"469\" data-end=\"480\">37 kJ/g</strong><span> </span>is the energy yield of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"504\" data-end=\"511\">fat</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"562\">\r\n<p data-start=\"515\" data-end=\"562\"><strong data-start=\"515\" data-end=\"526\">29 kJ/g</strong><span> </span>is the energy yield of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"561\">alcohol</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "10", "question": "১০) Which Bariatric surgery is most effective in weight reduction?", "option1": "ক) Gastric banding", "option2": "খ) Sleeve gastrectomy", "option3": "গ) Roux-en-Y gastric bypass", "option4": "ঘ) Duodenal switch", "answer": "4", "exp": "<p data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"473\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"130\">Duodenal switch (Biliopancreatic diversion with duodenal switch, BPD-DS)</strong><span> </span>is the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"207\">most effective bariatric procedure for long-term weight reduction</strong>. It combines a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"223\" data-end=\"245\">sleeve gastrectomy</strong><span> </span>with a marked degree of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"270\" data-end=\"291\">intestinal bypass</strong>, producing both<span> </span><strong data-start=\"308\" data-end=\"323\">restriction</strong><span> </span>and<span> </span><strong data-start=\"328\" data-end=\"345\">malabsorption</strong>. It results in the greatest percentage of excess weight loss but also carries the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"428\" data-end=\"472\" data-is-only-node=\"\">highest risk of nutritional deficiencies</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"475\" data-end=\"528\">The other options are less effective for weight loss:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"879\">\r\n<li data-start=\"529\" data-end=\"589\">\r\n<p data-start=\"531\" data-end=\"589\"><strong data-start=\"531\" data-end=\"550\">Gastric banding</strong><span> </span>– least effective; purely restrictive.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"590\" data-end=\"714\">\r\n<p data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"714\"><strong data-start=\"592\" data-end=\"614\">Sleeve gastrectomy</strong><span> </span>– effective restrictive procedure with hormonal benefits but less weight loss than duodenal switch.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"715\" data-end=\"879\">\r\n<p data-start=\"717\" data-end=\"879\"><strong data-start=\"717\" data-end=\"745\">Roux-en-Y gastric bypass</strong><span> </span>– highly effective and considered the standard bariatric procedure, but generally produces<span> </span><strong data-start=\"837\" data-end=\"878\">less weight loss than duodenal switch</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "11", "question": "১১) Deficiency of which vitamin causes Wernicke’s encephalopathy?", "option1": "Thiamine", "option2": "Riboflavin", "option3": "Niacin", "option4": "Biotin", "answer": "1", "exp": "<p data-start=\"68\" data-end=\"280\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"68\" data-end=\"97\">Wernicke's encephalopathy</strong><span> </span>is caused by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"111\" data-end=\"147\">thiamine (Vitamin B₁) deficiency</strong>, most commonly in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"166\" data-end=\"188\">chronic alcoholics</strong>, malnourished individuals, and patients with prolonged vomiting or after bariatric surgery.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"312\">The classic triad consists of:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"378\">\r\n<li data-start=\"313\" data-end=\"328\">\r\n<p data-start=\"315\" data-end=\"328\"><strong data-start=\"315\" data-end=\"328\">Confusion</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"329\" data-end=\"365\">\r\n<p data-start=\"331\" data-end=\"365\"><strong data-start=\"331\" data-end=\"365\">Ophthalmoplegia (or nystagmus)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"378\">\r\n<p data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"378\"><strong data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"378\">Ataxia</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"380\" data-end=\"533\">If left untreated, it may progress to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"418\" data-end=\"440\">Korsakoff syndrome</strong>, characterized by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"459\" data-end=\"532\">memory impairment, confabulation, and irreversible cognitive deficits</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"535\" data-end=\"575\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"576\" data-end=\"896\">\r\n<li data-start=\"576\" data-end=\"673\">\r\n<p data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"673\"><strong data-start=\"578\" data-end=\"605\">Riboflavin (Vitamin B₂)</strong><span> </span>deficiency causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"624\" data-end=\"672\">cheilosis, angular stomatitis, and glossitis</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"674\" data-end=\"764\">\r\n<p data-start=\"676\" data-end=\"764\"><strong data-start=\"676\" data-end=\"699\">Niacin (Vitamin B₃)</strong><span> </span>deficiency causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"718\" data-end=\"730\">pellagra</strong><span> </span>(dermatitis, diarrhea, dementia).</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"765\" data-end=\"896\">\r\n<p data-start=\"767\" data-end=\"896\"><strong data-start=\"767\" data-end=\"790\">Biotin (Vitamin B₇)</strong><span> </span>deficiency causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"809\" data-end=\"860\">dermatitis, alopecia, and neurological symptoms</strong>, but not Wernicke's encephalopathy.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "12", "question": "১২) Wilson’s disease occurs due to abnormal metabolism of which particle?", "option1": "Fe", "option2": "Cu", "option3": "I", "option4": "P", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"58\" data-end=\"346\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"58\" data-end=\"78\">Wilson's disease</strong><span> </span>is an<span> </span><strong data-start=\"85\" data-end=\"117\">autosomal recessive disorder</strong><span> </span>caused by mutations in the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"159\">ATP7B gene</strong>, leading to<span> </span><strong data-start=\"172\" data-end=\"202\">impaired copper metabolism</strong>. Defective biliary excretion of copper results in its accumulation in the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"277\" data-end=\"345\">liver, brain (especially the basal ganglia), cornea, and kidneys</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"348\" data-end=\"380\">Characteristic features include:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"381\" data-end=\"584\">\r\n<li data-start=\"381\" data-end=\"431\">\r\n<p data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"431\"><strong data-start=\"383\" data-end=\"408\">Chronic liver disease</strong><span> </span>(hepatitis, cirrhosis)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"432\" data-end=\"510\">\r\n<p data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"510\"><strong data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"465\">Neurological manifestations</strong><span> </span>(tremor, dystonia, dysarthria, parkinsonism)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"511\" data-end=\"541\">\r\n<p data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"541\"><strong data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"541\">Psychiatric disturbances</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"542\" data-end=\"584\">\r\n<p data-start=\"544\" data-end=\"584\"><strong data-start=\"544\" data-end=\"570\">Kayser–Fleischer rings</strong><span> </span>in the cornea</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"586\" data-end=\"626\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"627\" data-end=\"827\">\r\n<li data-start=\"627\" data-end=\"689\">\r\n<p data-start=\"629\" data-end=\"689\"><strong data-start=\"629\" data-end=\"642\">Fe (Iron)</strong><span> </span>metabolism is abnormal in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"669\" data-end=\"688\">hemochromatosis</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"690\" data-end=\"748\">\r\n<p data-start=\"692\" data-end=\"748\"><strong data-start=\"692\" data-end=\"706\">I (Iodine)</strong><span> </span>is involved in thyroid hormone synthesis.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"749\" data-end=\"827\">\r\n<p data-start=\"751\" data-end=\"827\"><strong data-start=\"751\" data-end=\"769\">P (Phosphorus)</strong><span> </span>metabolism is not primarily affected in Wilson's disease.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "13", "question": "১৩) Zinc is an essential component of following enzymes excluding ____?", "option1": "carbonic anhydrase", "option2": "alcohol dehydrogenase", "option3": "alkaline phosphatase", "option4": "aspartate aminotransferase", "answer": "4", "exp": "<p data-start=\"79\" data-end=\"174\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"79\" data-end=\"87\">Zinc</strong><span> </span>is an essential trace element that acts as a cofactor for numerous enzymes, including:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"175\" data-end=\"263\">\r\n<li data-start=\"175\" data-end=\"202\">\r\n<p data-start=\"177\" data-end=\"202\"><strong data-start=\"177\" data-end=\"199\">Carbonic anhydrase</strong><span> </span>✔️</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"203\" data-end=\"233\">\r\n<p data-start=\"205\" data-end=\"233\"><strong data-start=\"205\" data-end=\"230\">Alcohol dehydrogenase</strong><span> </span>✔️</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"234\" data-end=\"263\">\r\n<p data-start=\"236\" data-end=\"263\"><strong data-start=\"236\" data-end=\"260\">Alkaline phosphatase</strong><span> </span>✔️</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"265\" data-end=\"437\"><strong data-start=\"265\" data-end=\"301\">Aspartate aminotransferase (AST)</strong><span> </span>is<span> </span><strong data-start=\"305\" data-end=\"336\">not a zinc-dependent enzyme</strong>. It requires<span> </span><strong data-start=\"350\" data-end=\"391\">pyridoxal phosphate (PLP, Vitamin B₆)</strong><span> </span>as its coenzyme for transamination reactions.</p>"}, {"id": "14", "question": "১৪) Which vitamin is not synthesized in our body?", "option1": "vit K", "option2": "vit B12", "option3": "vit A", "option4": "vit D", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"70\" data-end=\"261\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"70\" data-end=\"85\">Vitamin B₁₂</strong><span> </span>is<span> </span><strong data-start=\"89\" data-end=\"126\">not synthesized by the human body</strong>. It is produced only by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"151\" data-end=\"169\">microorganisms</strong><span> </span>and must be obtained from<span> </span><strong data-start=\"196\" data-end=\"220\">animal-derived foods</strong><span> </span>(meat, fish, eggs, milk) or supplements.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"263\" data-end=\"303\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"304\" data-end=\"613\">\r\n<li data-start=\"304\" data-end=\"404\">\r\n<p data-start=\"306\" data-end=\"404\"><strong data-start=\"306\" data-end=\"319\">Vitamin K</strong><span> </span>– synthesized by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"337\" data-end=\"360\">intestinal bacteria</strong><span> </span>(gut flora), in addition to dietary intake.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"405\" data-end=\"510\">\r\n<p data-start=\"407\" data-end=\"510\"><strong data-start=\"407\" data-end=\"420\">Vitamin A</strong><span> </span>– can be formed in the body from<span> </span><strong data-start=\"454\" data-end=\"483\">β-carotene (provitamin A)</strong><span> </span>obtained from plant foods.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"511\" data-end=\"613\">\r\n<p data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"613\"><strong data-start=\"513\" data-end=\"526\">Vitamin D</strong><span> </span>– synthesized in the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"548\" data-end=\"556\">skin</strong><span> </span>from 7-dehydrocholesterol under ultraviolet (UVB) light.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "15", "question": "১৫) Lack of vit D in adult causes ____?", "option1": "rickets", "option2": "osteomalacia", "option3": "osteoporosis", "option4": "osteosarcoma", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"59\" data-end=\"211\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"59\" data-end=\"93\">Vitamin D deficiency in adults</strong><span> </span>causes<span> </span><strong data-start=\"101\" data-end=\"117\">osteomalacia</strong>, a disorder characterized by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"147\" data-end=\"196\">defective mineralization of newly formed bone</strong>, resulting in:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"212\" data-end=\"301\">\r\n<li data-start=\"212\" data-end=\"231\">\r\n<p data-start=\"214\" data-end=\"231\">Diffuse bone pain</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"232\" data-end=\"258\">\r\n<p data-start=\"234\" data-end=\"258\">Proximal muscle weakness</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"259\" data-end=\"280\">\r\n<p data-start=\"261\" data-end=\"280\">Fragility fractures</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"281\" data-end=\"301\">\r\n<p data-start=\"283\" data-end=\"301\">Difficulty walking</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"303\" data-end=\"343\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"344\" data-end=\"628\">\r\n<li data-start=\"344\" data-end=\"407\">\r\n<p data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"407\"><strong data-start=\"346\" data-end=\"357\">Rickets</strong><span> </span>occurs in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"380\">children</strong><span> </span>before epiphyseal closure.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"408\" data-end=\"548\">\r\n<p data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"548\"><strong data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"426\">Osteoporosis</strong><span> </span>is characterized by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"447\" data-end=\"495\">reduced bone mass with normal mineralization</strong><span> </span>and is not primarily caused by vitamin D deficiency.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"549\" data-end=\"628\">\r\n<p data-start=\"551\" data-end=\"628\"><strong data-start=\"551\" data-end=\"567\">Osteosarcoma</strong><span> </span>is a malignant bone tumor unrelated to vitamin D deficiency.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "16", "question": "১৬) A person can be identified as obese when BMI exceeds ____ kg/m2?", "option1": "18.5", "option2": "25", "option3": "30", "option4": "40", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"55\" data-end=\"134\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\">According to the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"72\" data-end=\"107\">World Health Organization (WHO)</strong><span> </span>classification for adults:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"136\" data-end=\"319\">\r\n<li data-start=\"136\" data-end=\"167\">\r\n<p data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"167\"><strong data-start=\"138\" data-end=\"153\">&lt;18.5 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Underweight</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"168\" data-end=\"205\">\r\n<p data-start=\"170\" data-end=\"205\"><strong data-start=\"170\" data-end=\"189\">18.5–24.9 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Normal weight</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"206\" data-end=\"240\">\r\n<p data-start=\"208\" data-end=\"240\"><strong data-start=\"208\" data-end=\"227\">25.0–29.9 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Overweight</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"241\" data-end=\"272\">\r\n<p data-start=\"243\" data-end=\"272\"><strong data-start=\"243\" data-end=\"258\">≥30.0 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>–<span> </span><strong data-start=\"261\" data-end=\"272\">Obesity</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"273\" data-end=\"319\">\r\n<p data-start=\"275\" data-end=\"319\"><strong data-start=\"275\" data-end=\"290\">≥40.0 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Class III (morbid) obesity</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"321\" data-end=\"395\">Therefore, a person is considered<span> </span><strong data-start=\"355\" data-end=\"364\">obese</strong><span> </span>when the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"374\" data-end=\"394\">BMI is ≥30 kg/m²</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"397\" data-end=\"437\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"438\" data-end=\"600\">\r\n<li data-start=\"438\" data-end=\"483\">\r\n<p data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"483\"><strong data-start=\"440\" data-end=\"454\">18.5 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Lower limit of normal BMI.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"484\" data-end=\"543\">\r\n<p data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"543\"><strong data-start=\"486\" data-end=\"498\">25 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Threshold for<span> </span><strong data-start=\"515\" data-end=\"529\">overweight</strong>, not obesity.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"544\" data-end=\"600\">\r\n<p data-start=\"546\" data-end=\"600\"><strong data-start=\"546\" data-end=\"558\">40 kg/m²</strong><span> </span>– Defines<span> </span><strong data-start=\"569\" data-end=\"599\">morbid (Class III) obesity</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "17", "question": "১৭) Which one is not an essential amino acid?", "option1": "glycine", "option2": "lysine", "option3": "leucine", "option4": "histidine", "answer": "1", "exp": "<p data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"205\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"65\">Glycine</strong><span> </span>is a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"71\" data-end=\"99\">non-essential amino acid</strong><span> </span>because it can be synthesized by the human body and therefore does not need to be obtained from the diet.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"207\" data-end=\"255\">The other options are<span> </span><strong data-start=\"229\" data-end=\"254\">essential amino acids</strong>:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"256\" data-end=\"387\">\r\n<li data-start=\"256\" data-end=\"271\">\r\n<p data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"271\"><strong data-start=\"258\" data-end=\"268\">Lysine</strong><span> </span></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"272\" data-end=\"288\">\r\n<p data-start=\"274\" data-end=\"288\"><strong data-start=\"274\" data-end=\"285\">Leucine</strong><span> </span></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"289\" data-end=\"387\">\r\n<p data-start=\"291\" data-end=\"387\"><strong data-start=\"291\" data-end=\"304\">Histidine</strong> (essential, particularly during growth and also considered essential in adults)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"389\" data-end=\"440\"><strong data-start=\"389\" data-end=\"440\">Essential amino acids (mnemonic: PVT TIM HALL):</strong></p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"441\" data-end=\"650\">\r\n<li data-start=\"441\" data-end=\"460\">\r\n<p data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"460\"><strong data-start=\"443\" data-end=\"448\">P</strong>henylalanine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"461\" data-end=\"473\">\r\n<p data-start=\"463\" data-end=\"473\"><strong data-start=\"463\" data-end=\"468\">V</strong>aline</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"474\" data-end=\"490\">\r\n<p data-start=\"476\" data-end=\"490\"><strong data-start=\"476\" data-end=\"481\">T</strong>ryptophan</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"491\" data-end=\"506\">\r\n<p data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"506\"><strong data-start=\"493\" data-end=\"498\">T</strong>hreonine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"507\" data-end=\"523\">\r\n<p data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"523\"><strong data-start=\"509\" data-end=\"514\">I</strong>soleucine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"524\" data-end=\"540\">\r\n<p data-start=\"526\" data-end=\"540\"><strong data-start=\"526\" data-end=\"531\">M</strong>ethionine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"541\" data-end=\"556\">\r\n<p data-start=\"543\" data-end=\"556\"><strong data-start=\"543\" data-end=\"548\">H</strong>istidine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"557\" data-end=\"623\">\r\n<p data-start=\"559\" data-end=\"623\"><strong data-start=\"559\" data-end=\"564\">A</strong>rginine<span> </span><em data-start=\"572\" data-end=\"623\">(essential during growth/conditionally essential)</em></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"624\" data-end=\"637\">\r\n<p data-start=\"626\" data-end=\"637\"><strong data-start=\"626\" data-end=\"631\">L</strong>eucine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"638\" data-end=\"650\">\r\n<p data-start=\"640\" data-end=\"650\"><strong data-start=\"640\" data-end=\"645\">L</strong>ysine</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "18", "question": "১৮) Characteristic 'J' wave in ECG are found in ______?", "option1": "ক) hypothermia", "option2": "খ) hyperthermia", "option3": "গ) obesity", "option4": "ঘ) drowning", "answer": "1", "exp": "<p data-start=\"58\" data-end=\"364\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\">The<span> </span><strong data-start=\"62\" data-end=\"86\">J wave (Osborn wave)</strong><span> </span>is a characteristic ECG finding in<span> </span><strong data-start=\"122\" data-end=\"137\">hypothermia</strong>, especially when the<span> </span><strong data-start=\"159\" data-end=\"201\">core body temperature falls below 32°C</strong>. It appears as a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"219\" data-end=\"311\">positive deflection at the junction (J point) between the QRS complex and the ST segment</strong>, most prominently in the inferior and lateral leads.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"407\">Other ECG changes in hypothermia include:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"408\" data-end=\"548\">\r\n<li data-start=\"408\" data-end=\"431\">\r\n<p data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"431\"><strong data-start=\"410\" data-end=\"431\">Sinus bradycardia</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"432\" data-end=\"473\">\r\n<p data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"473\"><strong data-start=\"434\" data-end=\"473\">Prolonged PR, QRS, and QT intervals</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"474\" data-end=\"548\">\r\n<p data-start=\"476\" data-end=\"548\"><strong data-start=\"476\" data-end=\"499\">Atrial fibrillation</strong><span> </span>or ventricular arrhythmias in severe hypothermia</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"550\" data-end=\"590\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"591\" data-end=\"792\">\r\n<li data-start=\"591\" data-end=\"644\">\r\n<p data-start=\"593\" data-end=\"644\"><strong data-start=\"593\" data-end=\"609\">Hyperthermia</strong><span> </span>does not produce J (Osborn) waves.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"645\" data-end=\"706\">\r\n<p data-start=\"647\" data-end=\"706\"><strong data-start=\"647\" data-end=\"658\">Obesity</strong><span> </span>has no characteristic association with J waves.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"707\" data-end=\"792\">\r\n<p data-start=\"709\" data-end=\"792\"><strong data-start=\"709\" data-end=\"721\">Drowning</strong><span> </span>itself does not produce J waves unless accompanied by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"776\" data-end=\"791\">hypothermia</strong>.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "19", "question": "১৯) Which one is a non-starch polysaccharide?", "option1": "ক) lactose", "option2": "খ) maltodextrin", "option3": "গ) cellulose", "option4": "ঘ) sorbitol", "answer": "3", "exp": "<p data-start=\"56\" data-end=\"265\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"56\" data-end=\"69\">Cellulose</strong><span> </span>is a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"75\" data-end=\"110\">non-starch polysaccharide (NSP)</strong><span> </span>and is the principal component of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"145\" data-end=\"162\">dietary fiber</strong>. Humans lack the enzyme<span> </span><strong data-start=\"187\" data-end=\"200\">cellulase</strong>, so cellulose is<span> </span><strong data-start=\"218\" data-end=\"234\">not digested</strong><span> </span>in the gastrointestinal tract.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"267\" data-end=\"307\">The other options are incorrect because:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"308\" data-end=\"526\">\r\n<li data-start=\"308\" data-end=\"365\">\r\n<p data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"365\"><strong data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"321\">Lactose</strong><span> </span>– a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"326\" data-end=\"342\">disaccharide</strong><span> </span>(glucose + galactose).</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"366\" data-end=\"457\">\r\n<p data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"457\"><strong data-start=\"368\" data-end=\"384\">Maltodextrin</strong><span> </span>– a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"389\" data-end=\"423\">starch-derived glucose polymer</strong>, not a non-starch polysaccharide.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"458\" data-end=\"526\">\r\n<p data-start=\"460\" data-end=\"526\"><strong data-start=\"460\" data-end=\"472\">Sorbitol</strong><span> </span>– a<span> </span><strong data-start=\"477\" data-end=\"503\">sugar alcohol (polyol)</strong>, not a polysaccharide.</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}, {"id": "20", "question": "২০) The drug Orlistat is used in ____?", "option1": "ক) malnutrition", "option2": "খ) obesity", "option3": "গ) hypothermia", "option4": "ঘ) hyperthermia", "answer": "2", "exp": "<p data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"257\" class=\"PDq2pG_selectionAnchorContainer\"><strong data-start=\"54\" data-end=\"66\">Orlistat</strong><span> </span>is an<span> </span><strong data-start=\"73\" data-end=\"94\">anti-obesity drug</strong><span> </span>that promotes weight loss by<span> </span><strong data-start=\"124\" data-end=\"169\">inhibiting gastric and pancreatic lipases</strong>, thereby reducing the digestion and absorption of dietary fat by approximately<span> </span><strong data-start=\"249\" data-end=\"256\">30%</strong>.</p>\r\n<p data-start=\"259\" data-end=\"279\">It is indicated for:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"417\">\r\n<li data-start=\"280\" data-end=\"309\">\r\n<p data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"309\"><strong data-start=\"282\" data-end=\"309\">Obesity (BMI ≥30 kg/m²)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"310\" data-end=\"417\">\r\n<p data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"417\"><strong data-start=\"312\" data-end=\"351\">Overweight patients (BMI ≥27 kg/m²)</strong><span> </span>with obesity-related comorbidities (e.g., diabetes, hypertension)</p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"419\" data-end=\"450\">Common adverse effects include:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"451\" data-end=\"559\">\r\n<li data-start=\"451\" data-end=\"464\">\r\n<p data-start=\"453\" data-end=\"464\">Steatorrhea</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"465\" data-end=\"477\">\r\n<p data-start=\"467\" data-end=\"477\">Flatulence</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"478\" data-end=\"493\">\r\n<p data-start=\"480\" data-end=\"493\">Fecal urgency</p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"494\" data-end=\"559\">\r\n<p data-start=\"496\" data-end=\"559\">Reduced absorption of<span> </span><strong data-start=\"518\" data-end=\"559\">fat-soluble vitamins (A, D, E, and K)</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>\r\n<p data-start=\"561\" data-end=\"630\">The other options are incorrect because Orlistat is<span> </span><strong data-start=\"613\" data-end=\"625\">not used</strong><span> </span>for:</p>\r\n<ul data-start=\"631\" data-end=\"686\">\r\n<li data-start=\"631\" data-end=\"649\">\r\n<p data-start=\"633\" data-end=\"649\"><strong data-start=\"633\" data-end=\"649\">Malnutrition</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"650\" data-end=\"667\">\r\n<p data-start=\"652\" data-end=\"667\"><strong data-start=\"652\" data-end=\"667\">Hypothermia</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n<li data-start=\"668\" data-end=\"686\">\r\n<p data-start=\"670\" data-end=\"686\"><strong data-start=\"670\" data-end=\"686\">Hyperthermia</strong></p>\r\n</li>\r\n</ul>"}]}, "_source": "archive-question-subject", "_exam_id": 274, "custom_field": "", "exam_time": "420000", "is_omr": false, "omr_time": "7200000", "examname": "May 03, 2018", "type": "BCS-Health-Long", "payment": "BALANCE_NOT"}